+── 1 · THE FIXTURE IS PRODUCTION'S SHAPE, NOT AN APPROXIMATION ────────────────
+
+Production's default-privilege rule, read from pg_default_acl 2026-09-03
+12:29:14Z, SELECT only — ONE entry for public functions, granted by postgres:
+
+    {anon=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres,service_role=X/postgres}
+
+installed on the fixture as:
+
+    ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
+      GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTIONS TO anon, authenticated, service_role;
+
+Both target functions were then created under it. The ACL they were BORN with:
+
+    recompute_entry_from_tag_assignments(uuid)
+      {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres,anon=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres,service_role=X/postgres}
+    search_certificates(text,text,date)
+      {=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres,anon=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres,service_role=X/postgres}
+
+That is BYTE-FOR-BYTE the ACL measured on production for both functions at
+2026-09-03 12:28:13Z. The fixture is not a model of production's grant posture;
+it is the same posture, arrived at the same way. F-66 in one line: the rule ADDS
+TO PostgreSQL's built-in default rather than replacing it, so the leading
+`=X/postgres` — PUBLIC — is there from birth.
+
+── 2 · HOW THE DEFECT VARIANTS WERE MADE ──────────────────────────────────────
+
+Each negative control is the REAL migration file with ONE line changed by sed.
+Nothing was hand-written, so the verification block under test is provably the
+shipped one:
+
+    no_public : sed '/^REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION.*FROM PUBLIC;$/d'
+    no_anon   : sed '/^REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION.*FROM anon;$/d'
+    too_wide  : sed 's/^\(REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION.*\) FROM anon;$/\1 FROM anon, authenticated;/'
+
+sha256 of the DO $verify$ block, extracted from each file with awk:
