+# P1 opener — `REVOKE ... FROM PUBLIC` on the two functions F-62 identified
+
+**Unit** Phase 1 opener · P31 `search_certificates`, P32 `recompute_entry_from_tag_assignments`
+**Author** D1 (Database & Runtime), with the security reviewer's reading applied
+**Lanes** production `jtdtehuqtinjxropkkcn` **SELECT only** · staging not measured · **no grant altered on either lane**
+**Fixture** scratch PostgreSQL 16.13 in the D1 container — F-65: grant controls run on fixtures
+**Transcript** `docs/evidence/d1/P1/P1-fixture-transcript.txt`
+**Ref** branch `d1/P1-revoke-public-20260903`, cut from `origin/staging` `5cd5ba6`
+
+---
+
+## 1 · The headline is not the SQL. It is a live client call site.
+
+`search_certificates` is called from a **public, unauthenticated page**:
+
+```
+src/App.tsx:410              <Route path="/verify" element={<VerifyCertificate />} />   ← no auth guard
+src/pages/VerifyCertificate.tsx:101
+                             const { data, error } = await supabase.rpc("search_certificates", { ... });
+```
+
+`/verify` has two modes. Only one of them breaks:
+
+| mode | RPC | after this revoke |
+|---|---|---|
+| **by id** | `verify_certificate(_cert_id)` | **unaffected** — not in this unit |
+| **by details** (name / course / date) | `search_certificates(...)` | **closed to anon** |
+
+And the way it breaks is the part that matters. The handler is:
+
+```js
+if (error || !data || data.length === 0) { setNotFound(true); }
+```
+
+A `42501 permission denied` and an empty result set take the same branch. So after the revoke, an anonymous visitor searching for their own certificate by name is told **"Certificate not found"**. Not an error. Not a sign-in prompt. A confident, wrong answer about whether their certificate exists — which nobody will report as a bug, because it does not look like one.
+
+**Therefore: client first, database second.** D2 removes or gates the "search by details" mode, that lands, and only then is `20260910_0001` applied. Applying it first is a self-inflicted support incident.
+
+The revoke itself is right and P31 wants it: `search_certificates` takes a **person's name** and returns their certificates. Anonymous name-search is the enumeration class the phase exists to close. **The disagreement is about order, not about whether.**
+
+### 1.1 The method's own precondition is not met
+
+> *"In Phase 1, D2's call-site inventory is delivered and the revocation list frozen by the Auditor **before** you write the SQL."*
+
+As of **2026-09-03 12:30Z** on ref `5cd5ba6`: `docs/evidence/d2/` **does not exist**, and `docs/gates/` holds only `GATE_REGISTER.md` and `phase-0-kickoff.md`. There is no frozen revocation list.
+
+The two call sites above are **D1's own read-only measurement of `src/`**, not an inventory. D2's inventory remains the authority and if it disagrees, these files are re-cut. This is recorded rather than worked around, and it is why `20260910_0001` carries an acknowledgement guard that `20260910_0002` does not.
+
+---
+
+## 2 · `recompute_entry_from_tag_assignments` — the safe one, and why
+
+**No client call site.** Searched read-only across `src/`, `functions/`, `supabase/functions/`, `scripts/` at 12:30Z: the only occurrence outside `supabase/migrations/` is the generated TypeScript declaration at `src/integrations/supabase/types.ts:7027`. A type, not a call.
+
+**Two in-database callers, both harmless to this change.** Measured 12:34:01Z / 12:35:19Z:
+
+| caller | `SECURITY DEFINER` | owner |
+|---|---|---|
+| `trg_recompute_entry_after_tag_change()` | **yes** | `postgres` |
+| `get_judging_live_tag_progression_invariant_admin()` | **yes** | `postgres` |
+
+Both are definer functions owned by `postgres`, which owns the target. They call it **as their owner, not as the session role**, so revoking `anon` cannot break either one.
+
+**Incidental observation, not part of this unit:** `trg_recompute_entry_after_tag_change()` is named as a trigger function but **is attached to no table** — the `pg_trigger` join at 12:35:19Z returns nothing for it. Reported, not investigated; it belongs to whoever owns the judging flow.
+
+**Why it is worth revoking**, measured 12:28:13Z: `VOLATILE` + `SECURITY DEFINER` + anon-executable. That is the amplification class — an anonymous caller commands unbounded write work with RLS bypassed.
+
+---
+
+## 3 · Why each migration carries two REVOKEs, not one
+
+Both functions, production `pg_proc.proacl`, **12:28:13Z**:
+
+```
+{=X/postgres,postgres=X/postgres,anon=X/postgres,authenticated=X/postgres,service_role=X/postgres}
+```
+
+The leading `=X/postgres` is **PUBLIC**. Both are F-62 class **TRAP-BOTH** — `anon` holds a named grant *and* inherits one through PUBLIC:
+
+| statement | result |
+|---|---|
+| `REVOKE ... FROM anon` alone | succeeds · **anon still executes**, through PUBLIC |
+| `REVOKE ... FROM PUBLIC` alone | succeeds · **anon still executes**, through its named grant |
+| both | **closed** |
+
+Both single-revoke cases were run on the fixture and **shown aborting the migration's own verification block** before these files were accepted.
+
+### 3.1 `authenticated` keeps its grant, and that is checked rather than assumed
+
+`authenticated` holds a **named** grant on both functions, so `REVOKE ... FROM PUBLIC` does not take it away. The frozen Phase 1 list says revoke from `anon`; widening it to `authenticated` would exceed the authorisation, and **a revoke that exceeds its authorisation is how a security unit becomes an outage.**
+
+So each verification block asserts `authenticated` **still** holds EXECUTE and fails the migration if not. The negative control for that assertion — `REVOKE ... FROM anon, authenticated` — was run and caught.
+
+If the Owner decides logged-in name-search is also enumeration, that is a separate unit with its own client change. It is not smuggled in here.
+
+---
+
+## 4 · The verification is a `DO` block, and the SELECT is only the record
+
+The instruction was "each ending with the F-66 verification SELECT". Both files end with exactly that SELECT. **But a SELECT that returns a `verdict` column cannot stop an apply.** It reports; it does not control. Nothing reads it, the transaction commits, and a migration that left `anon` executing is recorded as successful — which is F-62's failure mode reproduced one level up.
+
